{#
  Migration 0051 — THE CHANGE REQUEST, AND THE THESIS.
  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5 (verbatim), §16 MI30 (finding S16); spec R-2, R-3;
  kernel plan ruling D2 (why the change request has to be a first-class gated subject).

  Making the change request a gated subject is what turns "the permit is a protected
  branch" into "THE REPOSITORY is a protected branch and the permit is one of its refs".
  A document-change branch that could merge while carrying an undispositioned
  weaken-over-blood finding would leave the whole diachronic claim resting on one subject
  kind, and the management-of-change audit would go back to being a retrospective report
  instead of a live enforcement surface.

  ── THIS TABLE IS A MIRROR, AND THE MIRROR IS THE POINT ───────────────────────────
  Everything structural here is the permit's structure again: projected counters, one
  named CHECK per counter, merge evidence, UNIQUE (cr_id, gate_epoch) as the epoch pin's
  foreign-key target. What differs is only what a change request IS — it targets a
  protected ref rather than isolating an asset, so `target_ref` replaces the permit's
  boundary machinery, and there is no parent link because a change request does not fork.

  ── R-3, AND WHY EVERY NAME HERE CARRIES A PREFIX ─────────────────────────────────
  Specification rule R-3 (Exhibit Uniqueness) requires a refusal-bearing name to be
  unique across the WHOLE schema, not merely within its table, because the exhibit name
  alone must identify the refusal without a qualifying table. `cr_gate_closed_when_merged`
  and `cr_ctr_nonneg` are therefore not stylistic; a second `ctr_nonneg` in the schema
  would make "refused by ctr_nonneg" a sentence that needs a footnote, and a footnote is
  exactly what an exhibit may not need. The counter constraint names come from the
  binding, which already spells them with the prefix; the housekeeping names are spelled
  with it here.

  ── ONE COLUMN FAMILY, DELIBERATELY ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  Same ruling as the permit and for the same reason: the merge gate's correctness
  argument is the MATERIALISED conflict, two column families of one row are two KV keys,
  and two writes to two keys do not collide. Splitting the counters out would un-weld
  anomaly A1 precisely where anomaly A12 (isolation silently downgraded) needs the
  welding most.

  ── WHAT THIS TEMPLATE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT EMIT ─────────────────────────────────
  §5.5 writes `ref_name STRING NOT NULL REFERENCES mainline.ref (ref_name)`. `ref` is a
  VERTICAL relation (MAINLINE migration 0026, authored band) and `change_request` is
  SUBSTRATE (MR-1/MR-2), so a substrate template may not name it: the rendered file would
  fail to apply against the reference binding with 42P01, which is outside the refusal
  taxonomy. `target_ref` is in the same position and additionally has no foreign key in
  the architecture at all — the branch a change request targets may legitimately not
  exist yet. Both columns keep NOT NULL and a stated-ness CHECK; a vertical that owns a
  `ref` relation adds the foreign key in its own authored band.
#}
{% set cr_subjects = subjects | selectattr('kind', 'equalto', 'change_request') | list %}
{% if cr_subjects | length != 1 %}
{# 0051 is the change request's migration number. A binding that declares no
   change_request subject, or two, has no rendering here. #}
{{ the_binding_must_declare_exactly_one_subject_of_kind_change_request }}
{% endif %}
{% set s = cr_subjects[0] %}
{#
  `offset_of` mirrors the permit template's map and is EMPTY on purpose: no change-request
  counter carries `offset_allowed` polarity in either binding, because the reading-rate
  floor is a property of a crew being exposed to a permit's obligations at the point of
  work, not of a document branch. If a binding ever declares one, the lookup below refuses
  the render rather than silently emitting a two-term CHECK where a three-term one was
  declared.
#}
{% set offset_of = {} %}
{% set merge_evidence_constraint = 'cr_merge_evidence' %}
{% set counter_columns = [] %}
{% for c in s.counters %}
{% set _ = counter_columns.append(c.column) %}
{% if c.polarity == 'offset_allowed' %}
{% set _ = counter_columns.append(offset_of[c.column]) %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
-- @file 0051_change_request.sql
{{ header(file='0051_change_request.sql',
          title='CREATE TABLE ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.' ~ s.table ~ ' — the document branch is gated too (finding S16)',
          rationale='The change request is a gated subject in exactly the sense the permit is, and that identity is the thesis: the repository is the protected branch and the permit is one of its refs. Its counters are projections, its four refusals are independently named under R-3, and UNIQUE (cr_id, gate_epoch) gives the epoch pin its foreign-key target, so a precursor arriving after a document merge is refused by referential integrity rather than by a policy someone can edit.',
          mi=['MI30', 'MI03', 'MI04'], i=['I02', 'I03']) }}
--
-- ── THE PROJECTED CROSS-ROW FACTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Written by a projection trigger from a declared authority relation, never by the
-- inserter (I02). A value a client supplies for one of these columns does not survive the
-- write, and absence of the authority row REFUSES rather than defaulting.
--
{% for column in counter_columns %}
-- @projects   {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }}.{{ column }}
{% endfor %}
-- @projects   {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }}.site_role
-- @on_missing raise
-- @maintained-by the projection band (functions 0100-0109, triggers 0120-0129)
--
-- Rendered SQL comments rather than template pragmas: the Authority Source Contract
-- (A-1) demands a keyed [[authority_source]] behind each pragma'd column, and a counter
-- is an aggregate over an [[obligation_source]], not a keyed lookup of one row. The
-- permit template carries the same note at length.
--
-- ── COLUMN NOTES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- target_ref   the protected branch this change request wants to merge INTO. Deliberately
--              unconstrained by a foreign key even in a vertical that owns a ref relation:
--              a change request may legitimately name a branch that does not exist yet,
--              and refusing that write would move a workflow decision into the schema.
-- head_seq     the event chain's head, derived in-transaction and defended by the CAS on
--              cr_event; never allocated by a generator that commits outside the
--              transaction, because a gap in this chain must MEAN tampering.
-- gate_epoch   increments iff the gate OPENS. Pinned by epoch_pin_cr once the change
--              request merges, after which no obligation can be attached to it at all.
--
-- ── NO parent_cr_id, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT ────────────────────────────────
-- A permit forks: the declared remedy for a post-completion fact is to suspend the issued
-- permit and open a CHILD whose gate is cleared afresh, so the permit carries a parent
-- link. A change request does not fork — the remedy for a merged document change is a new
-- change request against the new head, which is an ordinary row with its own ref lineage
-- in the commit DAG. Inventing a parent column here would create a second, weaker lineage
-- beside the one the repository already keeps.

CREATE TABLE {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} (
  {{ '%-16s'|format(s.id_column) }} UUID NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  {{ '%-16s'|format('site_id') }} UUID NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-16s'|format('site_role') }} NAME NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-16s'|format('external_ref') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-16s'|format('ref_name') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-16s'|format('target_ref') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-16s'|format(s.state_column) }} {{ binding.schema }}.subject_state NOT NULL DEFAULT 'draft',
  {{ '%-16s'|format('head_seq') }} INT8 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  {{ '%-16s'|format(s.epoch_column) }} INT8 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  {{ '%-16s'|format('merged_commit') }} BYTES NULL,
  {{ '%-16s'|format('opened_at') }} TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),

  -- ▼▼ PROJECTED CROSS-ROW FACTS. Trigger-written, never supplied. ▼▼
{% for column in counter_columns %}
  {{ '%-16s'|format(column) }} INT8 NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
{% endfor %}
  -- ▲▲

  CONSTRAINT cr_ctr_nonneg CHECK (
{% for column in counter_columns %}
    {{ column }} >= 0{{ ' AND' if not loop.last }}
{% endfor %}
  ),

  -- ▼▼ THE PRODUCT, MIRRORED. {{ (s.counters | length) + 1 }} independent refusals; each name is the exhibit. ▼▼
{% for c in s.counters %}
{% if c.polarity == 'offset_allowed' %}
  CONSTRAINT {{ c.constraint }}
      CHECK ({{ s.state_column }} <> '{{ s.completing_state }}'
             OR {{ c.column }} = 0
             OR {{ offset_of[c.column] }} > 0),
{% else %}
  CONSTRAINT {{ '%-34s'|format(c.constraint) }} CHECK ({{ s.state_column }} <> '{{ s.completing_state }}' OR {{ c.column }} = 0),
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
  CONSTRAINT {{ '%-34s'|format(merge_evidence_constraint) }} CHECK ({{ s.state_column }} <> '{{ s.completing_state }}' OR merged_commit IS NOT NULL),
  -- ▲▲

  CONSTRAINT cr_ledger_nonneg       CHECK (head_seq >= 0 AND {{ s.epoch_column }} >= 0),
  CONSTRAINT cr_external_ref_stated CHECK (external_ref <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT cr_ref_name_stated     CHECK (ref_name <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT cr_target_ref_stated   CHECK (target_ref <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT cr_site_role_stated    CHECK (site_role <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT cr_commit_sized        CHECK (merged_commit IS NULL OR length(merged_commit) = 32),
  CONSTRAINT pk_change_request PRIMARY KEY ({{ s.id_column }}),
  -- The epoch pin's foreign-key target, mirroring the permit's. epoch_pin_cr is declared
  -- against this UNIQUE in migration 0071b.
  CONSTRAINT cr_epoch_target        UNIQUE ({{ s.id_column }}, {{ s.epoch_column }}),
  CONSTRAINT cr_external_ref_unique UNIQUE (site_id, external_ref)
);
